WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.240
Amazon can’t stop lying.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.200
And this time, you might be the one paying for it.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.280
In August, the FTC and 22 states sued Amazon.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.000
Amazon had allegedly, secretly overcharged&nbsp;
the businesses that advertise on it.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:14.800
For years.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.880
By tens of billions of dollars.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.360
And internally, Amazon was “hoping&nbsp;
that advertisers don’t notice.”

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.760
The FTC says those costs were “largely&nbsp;
passed on to American consumers.”

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.840
But this is just the start.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.240
Drivers. Parents. Prime members.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.320
Amazon keeps getting caught.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:43.200
So why won’t they stop?

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.840
Let’s first look at the case with 22 states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.320
and even the FTC, going after Amazon.&nbsp;
Because this case is pretty baffling.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.520
To understand how they lied, exactly, and how&nbsp;
they charged so many people so much more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.160
we need to look at one of Amazon’s&nbsp;
biggest moneymakers: advertising.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.560
When you search for a product on Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.960
the sponsored spots you see at&nbsp;
the top are basically an auction.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.920
Different sellers compete for those spots&nbsp;
by bidding on keywords. So if you search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.960
for “speakers”, a whole bunch of speaker&nbsp;
products compete to get you to see them first.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:26.480
In 2025 alone, Amazon brought in $68.6 billion&nbsp;
in ad revenue. Most of it came from sponsored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.640
listings. That's more than double the $31&nbsp;
billion it made from advertising in 2021.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.400
What’s important is how much&nbsp;
Amazon charges the winner.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.200
Once they win… what do they pay?

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.120
Amazon has described these&nbsp;
as “second-price” auctions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.520
meaning the winner doesn’t have to pay their&nbsp;
full bid, just enough to beat the second price.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:57.520
So if you bid $10, but the next highest bidder&nbsp;
offers $7, you'd expect to pay around $7.01.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.360
It's similar to eBay. You enter&nbsp;
the maximum you're willing to pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.720
but the system only charges&nbsp;
you enough to stay ahead.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.640
But there’s another way to&nbsp;
do it: a first-price auction.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.400
In a first-price auction,&nbsp;
you pay exactly what you bid.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.560
So if you bid $10 and win, you pay $10,&nbsp;
even if the second-place bid is only $2.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.840
That means you're probably going to play it safe&nbsp;
and bid lower, because you don't want to overpay.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.120
But in a second-price auction, you can bid&nbsp;
closer to what the ad is actually worth to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.720
because you expect to pay only slightly&nbsp;
more than the next highest bidder.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.720
So people tend to place higher bids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.440
trusting that Amazon would only charge&nbsp;
just above the second-place bid…

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.160
…But what if they decided not to?

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.720
What if, the reason Amazon’s advertising revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.680
grew so fast… was because they weren’t&nbsp;
charging advertisers the second price?

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.240
"Many advertisers bid far higher than what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:56.640
are willing to pay … because they&nbsp;
assume [the auction is] a GSP."

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.040
Leaked internal documents suggest Amazon&nbsp;
knew advertisers were bidding as if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.920
were taking part in a true second-price auction.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.800
Amazon tested how much it could raise these&nbsp;
hidden charges without advertisers noticing.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.600
In 2021, advertisers paid their full&nbsp;
bids about 30 to 40% of the time.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:20.160
By 2022, that had risen to around&nbsp;
70%. And by 2024, it was about 80%.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.960
But surely someone would notice,&nbsp;
right? Well, to make it less obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.400
Amazon raised the surcharges gradually, so&nbsp;
advertisers wouldn't immediately notice the jump.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:33.600
Amazon allegedly pushed these&nbsp;
surcharges even higher during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.040
major shopping events like&nbsp;
Prime Day and Black Friday.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.080
Internally, Amazon discussed&nbsp;
raising prices while "hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.640
that advertisers don't notice&nbsp;
and decrease bids or ad spend."

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.840
But someone did notice, eventually.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:54.560
The FTC and 22 states sued Amazon on August&nbsp;
31, 2026, in federal court in Seattle.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.160
And what the FTC found… was truly baffling.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:07.120
This wasn’t an accident. Amazon&nbsp;
knew exactly what they were doing.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.600
The lawsuit accused Amazon of secretly inflating&nbsp;
prices in its online search advertising auctions.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.240
“Amazon has millions of advertising customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.680
who were misled into paying&nbsp;
significantly higher prices”

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.360
“Amazon has continually tested and&nbsp;
monitored its customers to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.640
its prices without getting caught, and,&nbsp;
to date, this scheme has been successful”

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.040
Amazon also “changed its auction&nbsp;
rules without notice by adding an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.080
undisclosed surcharge that Amazon referred&nbsp;
to internally as a ‘soft reserve price.’”

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.040
Basically, that soft reserve price meant Amazon&nbsp;
could add an extra charge to the auction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.200
pushing the final price closer to what&nbsp;
the advertiser had originally bid.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.560
In fact, the head of Amazon&nbsp;
Ads allegedly described it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.160
a "proxy 2nd price" that Amazon calculated itself.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.520
And apparently, Amazon didn't just stop there.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.520
When advertisers started&nbsp;
asking questions, Amazon lied.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.320
Meanwhile, leaked internal documents revealed&nbsp;
that Amazon warned staff against revealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.040
the surcharges because it could cause&nbsp;
"irrevocable damage to advertiser trust."

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:21.600
Even in 2024, senior executives allegedly&nbsp;
described the system as a "clever non-transparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.560
way to charge first price”, and that it was an&nbsp;
“incredibly effective way to drive revenue.”

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.120
Yeah, I’m sure it was, Amazon.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.720
One employee even admitted:

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.480
"The benefit to Amazon comes&nbsp;
at the cost of advertisers."

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.600
Of course, Amazon disputed the FTC's case.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.280
The company says advertisers&nbsp;
never pay more than their own bid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.200
and that the cost per click stayed flat after&nbsp;
adjusting for inflation between 2019 and 2024.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.200
To Amazon, the FTC cherry-picked&nbsp;
old training materials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.480
including one video that was only viewed&nbsp;
by 928 people over two and a half years.

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.000
But, what the FTC found is pretty cut and dry.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.920
You might think, “Okay, but this is just&nbsp;
advertisers. Who cares if they pay more?”

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.280
Well, it turns out, it could&nbsp;
very well be you as well.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.160
These advertisers include more than&nbsp;
500,000 small and medium-sized businesses.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.120
The FTC estimates the alleged overcharges&nbsp;
could amount to tens of billions of dollars.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.920
According to FTC Chairman Andrew Ferguson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.600
“These higher costs were largely&nbsp;
passed on to American consumers.”

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.320
Amazon, of course, disagreed again, and&nbsp;
said none of those costs reached shoppers.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.320
The lawsuit is still ongoing. The&nbsp;
FTC and states are asking the court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.680
to stop the practice and to make Amazon pay&nbsp;
penalties and give money back to advertisers.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.600
Texas is also pursuing a separate&nbsp;
case over the same conduct.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.760
But, this is far from the first time&nbsp;
Amazon’s done something like this.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.640
Amazon can’t seem to stop lying, and they have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.520
pattern of misleading people about where&nbsp;
their money goes, like with Amazon Flex.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:59.840
Flex is basically Uber for Amazon&nbsp;
deliveries. These drivers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.240
gig workers who use their own cars to&nbsp;
deliver Amazon packages and groceries.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:07.120
To attract more drivers, Amazon advertised that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.080
Flex drivers could earn $18 to $25&nbsp;
an hour, plus 100% of their tips.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.360
Sounds pretty good!

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:18.320
And to win over customers, Amazon promised&nbsp;
their tips would go entirely to the drivers.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.440
But in late 2016, Amazon quietly lowered what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:25.040
it paid drivers and used their&nbsp;
tips to cover the difference.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.400
“Amazon allegedly received “hundreds” of&nbsp;
complaints as drivers began to become suspicious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.760
but emails were sent to them “falsely” claiming&nbsp;
that no changes had been made to the tip system”

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.480
Amazon also only stopped after learning&nbsp;
of the FTC's investigation in 2019.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:41.680
Very suspicious.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:46.800
The FTC revealed Amazon had&nbsp;
withheld $61.7 million in tips.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.200
And eventually, Amazon settled.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.520
Fun fact, DoorDash did something similar&nbsp;
around the same time. And used customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.320
tips to cover its own share of drivers' pay&nbsp;
instead of adding them on top. That meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:02.720
the more a customer tipped, the less&nbsp;
DoorDash itself had to pay the driver.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.720
They were also sued.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.800
But, back to Amazon.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:11.520
This next deception, might be way&nbsp;
worse, for a different reason…

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:17.040
In 2019, a coalition of 21 children's,&nbsp;
consumer, and privacy groups filed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.000
complaint with the FTC, accusing Amazon&nbsp;
of violating children's privacy law.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.200
They said Amazon was keeping&nbsp;
children's Alexa voice recordings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.120
indefinitely unless parents went&nbsp;
out of their way to delete them.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.680
The FTC later found that Amazon had&nbsp;
kept those recordings indefinitely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.040
even after parents asked for them to be deleted.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:39.280
And it gets worse.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:45.280
Even when Amazon did delete the recordings,&nbsp;
the FTC said it often kept the transcripts.

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.480
Why? To improve Alexa's voice recognition.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.200
Yeah…

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.400
And as always, Amazon denied these claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:55.920
but still agreed to pay $25&nbsp;
million to settle the case.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.600
But the money wasn't the only&nbsp;
thing Amazon had to give up.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.640
The company also had to delete&nbsp;
inactive child accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.440
children's voice recordings, and location data.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.720
And Amazon was barred from using&nbsp;
data that people had asked it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.120
delete to build or improve any product.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:11.440
Great.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.880
Oh, fun fact, that same week Ring, Amazon's&nbsp;
doorbell company, was hit with a separate case.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:19.760
The FTC found out that Ring&nbsp;
had allowed employees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.080
contractors to watch customers' private videos.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.760
And that one cost Amazon another $5.8 million.

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:28.160
And we're still not done.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:31.760
Let’s talk about one of&nbsp;
Amazon’s biggest deceptions.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.400
They deceived tens of millions of customers&nbsp;
into paying for services they didn’t even want.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:40.960
And even tried to delay and interfere&nbsp;
with the FTC’s investigation.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:52.960
Let’s talk about Amazon Prime.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:54.800
Since you’ve watched this far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.200
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:03.840
When you're looking at an item, Amazon would&nbsp;
show you something like "get FREE two-day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:09.680
shipping." But this is secretly a signup button&nbsp;
for Prime, with an auto-renewing subscription.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.200
Many users signed up for Prime&nbsp;
without actually knowing it.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.800
When you go to purchase something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:18.560
Amazon will first ask you to sign&nbsp;
up for Prime before you can finish.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:23.200
It's harder to buy the item without also&nbsp;
subscribing to Prime than signing up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:28.480
with the signup being a massive button,&nbsp;
and the other a tiny blue link elsewhere.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:35.200
The FTC said "Amazon used manipulative, coercive,&nbsp;
or deceptive user-interface designs known as 'dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:40.560
patterns' to trick consumers into enrolling in&nbsp;
automatically-renewing Prime subscriptions."

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.440
The FTC said some checkout buttons didn't make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.680
clear that clicking them would also&nbsp;
sign you up for a Prime subscription.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.400
One button even said, "No, I don't&nbsp;
want Free Shipping," which was equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.240
to saying no to signing up&nbsp;
for Prime… pretty deceptive.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.320
But it gets way worse.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.240
Signing up was easy, but canceling? Oh boy.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.160
First, you have to find the place to cancel&nbsp;
Prime, which is the "cancellation flow",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.880
something Amazon made intentionally difficult.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:14.160
Then, you are redirected to multiple pages,&nbsp;
where Amazon gives you offer after offer.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.520
A discounted price.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.680
The option to just turn off auto-renew.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.760
The option not to cancel.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.000
Only after navigating several different pages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:25.840
could you finally cancel—if you&nbsp;
figured out how to get there.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.560
On mobile, it's even longer.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.520
By the 6th page on mobile, Amazon says&nbsp;
"Thank you for being a member with us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:37.280
Take a look back at your journey with&nbsp;
Prime," then presents three options.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.280
"Keep My Benefits," "Continue to&nbsp;
Cancel," and "Remind Me Later."

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.120
None of these options ended the Prime&nbsp;
membership. Consumers who selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:48.720
"Continue to Cancel" proceeded&nbsp;
to a seventh [of eight] page.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:54.880
For Amazon, the desired action is signing up,&nbsp;
and the undesired action is you unsubscribing.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.960
The more friction a process has,&nbsp;
the less likely you are to do it.

00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:02.800
Amazon executives and employees knew&nbsp;
about these unwanted subscriptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:08.000
and discussed them internally, with one&nbsp;
employee calling it "an unspoken cancer."

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:08.960
Yikes.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:12.960
Another described "subscription&nbsp;
driving" as "a bit of a shady world."

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:17.200
In fact, Amazon had been manipulating&nbsp;
customers for years, and also "attempted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:21.920
to delay and hinder the Commission's&nbsp;
investigation in multiple instances."

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:27.440
Companies can hold back communications with their&nbsp;
lawyers, known as attorney-client privilege.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.000
But, Amazon claimed this for documents&nbsp;
that weren’t even legal communications.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.120
The FTC said Amazon had wrongly&nbsp;
withheld about 92% of the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:41.200
documents by labeling them as privileged. Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:46.000
“purposefully abused privilege claims to&nbsp;
keep inculpatory documents from the FTC.”

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.920
The judge gave the FTC more days to&nbsp;
gather evidence, barred Amazon from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.880
using documents it had handed over late, and&nbsp;
ordered Amazon to pay the FTC’s legal fees.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.600
The same judge also said Amazon’s withholding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.960
thousands of documents was&nbsp;
“tantamount to bad faith.”

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.640
So, what happened? What came of all of this?

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:09.600
Amazon said they didn't do anything&nbsp;
wrong, and always followed the law.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.640
Sure.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:16.800
In 2022, Amazon agreed to let customers in the EU&nbsp;
cancel Prime in just two clicks, after consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.320
groups complained about confusing menus and&nbsp;
repeated attempts to keep people subscribed.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.960
There is some more good news with this case too!

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:29.440
The court has now raised the&nbsp;
maximum refund from $51 to $200,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:35.200
and as of September 2026, Amazon had&nbsp;
already paid out more than $845 million.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:41.280
This is a fairly big settlement, but, most of the&nbsp;
cases are just pennies to a company like Amazon.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.480
Amazon’s gross profit for 2026, was $360 billion.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.040
Not revenue, gross profit.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.720
If they get caught, they just&nbsp;
pay the settlement, and carry on.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:55.120
It’s simply a cost of doing business to them.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:59.920
This is why Amazon can’t stop lying.&nbsp;
Because they aren’t held accountable.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.240
But there is something interesting.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:08.080
Europe investigated another issue, over use of&nbsp;
data of competitors, and made Amazon legally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.840
promise to stop, with fines of up to 10% of&nbsp;
its annual turnover if it breaks that promise.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:18.800
If we want Amazon to stop lying, they&nbsp;
need to be held accountable when they do.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.960
Have you also noticed that Amazon&nbsp;
these days… feels pretty awful?

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.760
I've basically stopped&nbsp;
shopping there almost entirely.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:29.760
It feels like most of it is&nbsp;
overpriced junk from Alibaba.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:35.440
And I think, Amazon’s own advertising system,&nbsp;
has caused the platform to be filled with junk.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.760
To find out how, watch this video.

